# ── Hardware control — universal power modes, fan curves, RGB ────
# (Agent AH, 2026-08-12 — Hardware app's platform-profile/fan/RGB scope)
#
# dkms: explicit even though nvidia-open-dkms above already pulls it in as a
# hard dependency (`pacman -Qi nvidia-open-dkms` -> Depends On: dkms, and it
# was in fact already present on the dev/preview machine for exactly that
# reason). Listing it here documents the OTHER, unrelated reason it must
# never quietly disappear: the community msi-ec driver (real EC-level fan
# curves + RGB "shift" modes on MSI laptops — AUR-only, opt-in, see
# docs/MSI_EC_OPT_IN_2026-08-12.md) is itself an out-of-tree module and MUST
# be rebuilt against every new kernel. Without dkms a routine `pacman -Syu`
# silently breaks fan control with nothing on screen announcing it — the
# module just stops matching the running kernel until someone thinks to
# check `lsmod`.
dkms
# openrgb: nyxus_control.py's RGB tab has probed for this binary since it
# shipped (`which openrgb`) but it was never in this list, so the tab always
# rendered its color/mode buttons pointed at a program that did not exist on
# disk — one of the three known instances of this build shipping a control
# with nothing behind it. Packaging it is half the fix; the other half is
# nyxus_control.py's _rgb_probe(), which now hides the RGB tab entirely
# unless `openrgb --list-devices` finds at least one real device, so an
# installed-but-deviceless machine does not get a tab full of dead buttons
# either — measured live on the dev/preview machine: openrgb IS present
# there and still finds zero devices, and the tab is correctly absent.
openrgb

# ── Bluetooth + printing + webcam ───────────────────────────────
bluez-obex
cups
cups-pdf
system-config-printer
v4l-utils
# WIP-120. cups + hplip cover driverless IPP Everywhere, which is most printers
# sold recently. These two are the PPD databases anything older needs. The
# failure without them is undramatic and hard to diagnose: the printer is
# detected, it appears in the dialog, and the output is wrong or absent.
gutenprint
foomatic-db
# WIP-119. simple-scan and sane were both packaged and there was a scanning
# surface — with no backend that can find a modern scanner. Nearly every
# scanner sold in the last decade is driverless, reached over eSCL/AirScan or
# WSD rather than by a vendor SANE backend, and sane-airscan is what speaks
# those. Without it simple-scan opens, finds nothing, and reports no scanner
# attached on a machine with a working scanner on the same network — a
# false-green surface. ipp-usb covers the USB half, where the same driverless
# protocol runs over a USB link.
sane-airscan
ipp-usb

# ── Biometric + hardware-token authentication ───────────────────
# fprintd  → fingerprint daemon (libfprint backend; works with most
#            laptop fingerprint sensors out of the box).
# pam-u2f  → PAM module for FIDO2/U2F hardware tokens (YubiKey).
# libfido2 → FIDO2 support library used by pam-u2f + ykman.
# yubikey-manager → CLI to register/program YubiKeys.
# python-opencv + python-dlib are runtime deps for `howdy` face auth
# (built from AUR by customize_airootfs.sh, since howdy is AUR-only).
fprintd
libfprint
pam-u2f
libfido2
yubikey-manager
# pamtester is not packaged and is not built at bake time. It existed
# only for nyxus-bd-router (NYXUS backdoor, WIP-324). USB 08-26 SYS
# dropped the customize curl.
python-opencv
# python-dlib removed 2026-05-11 — AUR-only, pulled in transitively by
# howdy's own debian/install.sh during the customize_airootfs.sh build.
# v4l2loopback-dkms — virtual V4L2 loopback device kernel module.
# Provides the v4l2loopback kernel module across the shipped kernel set.
v4l2loopback-dkms
# oath-toolkit ships oathtool (TOTP code generator/verifier) and pam_oath.
# Used by the backdoor stack as the 4th factor (rotating 30-second OTP).
oath-toolkit
# qrencode renders the TOTP enrolment URI as an ASCII QR code so the user
# can scan it into Aegis/Authy/Google Authenticator from a terminal.
qrencode

